Hello undici team,

First of all, thank you for this fantastic, high-performance HTTP client!

## This would solve...

Currently, when sending a request with a `FormData` body, `undici` generates a `multipart/form-data` boundary with a hardcoded prefix: 

https://github.com/nodejs/undici/blob/032d20081baa986493a5b0929cf0831dd72cfc9e/lib/web/fetch/body.js#L121-L122
https://github.com/nodejs/undici/blob/032d20081baa986493a5b0929cf0831dd72cfc9e/lib/web/fetch/body.js#L188

This behavior, while functional, acts as a clear fingerprint, making it trivial for a server to identify that the request was sent using `undici`. For applications that require a degree of anonymity, perform web scraping, or simply wish to avoid revealing their technology stack for security or policy reasons, this fingerprint is undesirable.

## The implementation should look like...

The implementation should involve extending the `undici` `FormData` class with methods to allow developers to set and get a custom boundary. This approach ties the boundary directly to the data it describes, which is semantically correct.

A proposed API would be:

```javascript
import { request, FormData } from 'undici';

// 1. Create a FormData instance
const formData = new FormData();
formData.append('key', 'value');

// 2. Define and set a custom boundary
const myBoundary = `----WebKitFormBoundaryRandomChars12345`;
formData.setBoundary(myBoundary);

// (Optional) A getter method would also be useful for inspection
console.log(formData.getBoundary()); // -> '----WebKitFormBoundaryRandomChars12345'

// 3. The request function requires no changes
const { headers } = await request('https://example.com', {
  method: 'POST',
  body: formData,
});

// The resulting 'content-type' header should now use the custom boundary:
// 'multipart/form-data; boundary=----WebKitFormBoundaryRandomChars12345'
```

If `setBoundary()` is never called on a `FormData` instance, `undici` should use its current default generation logic (`----formdata-undici-...`) to ensure the change is non-breaking and fully backward-compatible.

## I have also considered...

The only viable alternative to achieve this is to manually replicate undici's internal encoding logic.

This involves copying the source code responsible for processing a FormData object from the undici repository directly into my project and modifying the line where the boundary is generated.

This approach is highly undesirable as it creates a significant maintenance burden. My local copy of the code would become brittle and would not receive any future bug fixes, performance improvements, or security patches made to the original undici implementation. It forces developers to resort to code duplication, which is a well-known anti-pattern, simply to change one hardcoded string.

## Additional context

Implementing this feature by extending the FormData class would offer a powerful, elegant, and integrated solution, preventing the need for developers to maintain fragile forks or duplicates of internal code. 

Thank you for your consideration.

